<worksheet xml:id="ws-product-rule">
  <title>The Product Rule</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the product rule for differentiating a product of two functions.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Apply the product rule to compute derivatives of products involving polynomials, trigonometric, and exponential functions.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Recognize when the product rule is needed, and when it is not.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <page>
    <p>
      <xref ref="block-objectives" text="custom">📋 <term><![CDATA[<objectives>]]> Block</term></xref>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      When a function is built by multiplying two simpler functions, we cannot just multiply their derivatives. The <term>product rule</term> tells us what to do instead.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-product-rule">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable functions, then
          <me>
            \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr]
            = f'(x)\,g(x) + f(x)\,g'(x).
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <xref ref="block-definition" text="custom">📋 <term><![CDATA[<definition>]]> Block</term></xref>
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
      <title>Why we need it</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = x^3</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compute <m>f'(x)\,g'(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>f'(x)\,g'(x) = (2x)(3x^2) = 6x^3</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>f(x)\,g(x)</m> first, then differentiate the result to find 
            <md>\frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr]</md>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>f(x)\,g(x) = x^5</m>, so 
            <md>\frac{d}{dx}\bigl[f(x)\,g(x)\bigr] = 5x^4</md>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Compare your two answers. Is <m>(fg)' = f'g'</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The answers do not match, so <m>(fg)' \neq f'g'</m>. We need a different rule.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <example xml:id="ex-applying-product-rule">
      <title>Applying the product rule</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of <m>h(x) = x^2 \sin(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>g(x) = \sin(x)</m>, so <m>f'(x) = 2x</m> and <m>g'(x) = \cos(x)</m>. By <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>,
          <me>
            h'(x) = f'(x)\,g(x) + f(x)\,g'(x)
                  = 2x\sin(x) + x^2 \cos(x).
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise workspace="2.5in">
      <title>Practice</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use the product rule (see <xref ref="def-product-rule"/>) to find the derivative of each function.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>p(x) = x^3 e^x</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>p'(x) = 3x^2 e^x + x^3 e^x</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q(x) = (2x + 1)\cos(x)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>q'(x) = 2\cos(x) - (2x+1)\sin(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>r(x) = \sqrt{x}\,\ln(x)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>r'(x) = \dfrac{\ln(x)}{2\sqrt{x}} + \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x}}</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>s(x) = (x^2 + 3)(x^3 - 2x)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>s'(x) = 2x(x^3 - 2x) + (x^2+3)(3x^2 - 2)</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-product-rule-mc">
      <title>Spot the derivative</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Which of the following is the derivative of <m>h(x) = x^2 \sin(x)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h'(x) = 2x \cos(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It is tempting to take the derivative directly like this, but the correct approach is
              <md>(fg)' = f'g + fg'</md>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h'(x) = 2x\sin(x) + x^2\cos(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              With <m>f = x^2</m> and <m>g = \sin(x)</m>, the product rule gives
              <md>f'g + fg' = 2x\sin(x) + x^2\cos(x)</md>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h'(x) = 2x\sin(x) - x^2\cos(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Close, but the product rule adds the two terms, not subtracts them.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h'(x) = x^2\cos(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This differentiates only the <m>\sin(x)</m> factor and leaves <m>x^2</m> alone. The product rule requires differentiating each factor in turn: <m>f'g + fg'</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>